---
import '@fontsource-variable/inter';
import Navbar from '../navbar/navbar.astro';
import results from '../../../public/embodied-rsi/data/results.json';
const fixed = (n: number) => n.toFixed(2);
import '../../styles/global.css';
import '../../styles/embodiedrsi.css';
const stages = [
  { title: 'Experience', word: 'Explore through code.', text: 'The coding agent writes and executes Python in a persistent robot session. It observes what happened, revises the next segment, and can reset in the Playground.', file: 'submission/solution.py', number: '01' },
  { title: 'Reflect', word: 'Turn feedback into knowledge.', text: 'Images, execution output, and the task-success signal ground the next revision. Skills retain control procedures and their usage conditions. Lessons preserve observed failures, constraints, and recovery experience.', file: 'skills/ + lessons/', number: '02' },
  { title: 'Re-enter', word: 'Bring experience to the next attempt.', text: 'The same coding agent can consult and revise its accumulated skills and lessons during further exploration. The evolving memory closes the Playground loop.', file: 'Hₖ → Hₖ₊₁', number: '03' },
  { title: 'Transfer', word: 'Freeze the experience. Start fresh.', text: 'After exploration, seal skills and lessons. Each Test scene starts a fresh agent and simulator. The agent can observe, code, and recover within that scene, with no reset or cross-scene learning.', file: 'frozen H → new scenes', number: '04' },
];
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#f6f5f1" />
  <meta name="description" content="EmbodiedEvo turns physical interaction into reusable Skills and Lessons. On 42 RoboDojo tasks, frozen experience raises Astra’s paired success rate from 8.50% to 30.17%." />
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex,nofollow" />
  <meta property="og:title" content="EmbodiedEvo: Self-improving Code Agents for Physical Intelligence" />
  <meta property="og:description" content="Learn through physical interaction. Retain control knowledge. Pass it to a fresh agent. Results on 42 RoboDojo simulation tasks." />
  <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
  <meta property="og:image" content="/embodied-rsi/figures/embodied-rsi-overview.webp" />
  <meta property="og:image:alt" content="EmbodiedEvo Playground and Test overview" />
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/embodied-rsi/mark.svg" />
  <title>EmbodiedEvo: Self-improving Code Agents for Physical Intelligence</title>
</head>
<body>
<a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
<Navbar linkBase="https://lab.einsia.ai" activePath="/research" />
<main id="main">
<section class="hero container" aria-labelledby="project-title">
  <div class="hero-meta"><span class="eyebrow"><i class="status-dot"></i> EMBODIED INTELLIGENCE THROUGH CODE</span><span class="mono">OCTOBER 2026</span></div>
  <div class="hero-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow hero-eyebrow">PHYSICAL INTERACTION → REUSABLE CONTROL KNOWLEDGE</p>
    <h1 id="project-title">Embodied<span>Evo</span><span class="title-orbit" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></h1>
    <p class="hero-tagline">Self-improving Code Agents<br />for <em>Physical Intelligence</em></p>
    <p class="hero-description">Coding agents learn from physical feedback, retain Skills and Lessons,<br class="desktop-break" /> and pass that experience to fresh agents solving new scenes.<br class="desktop-break" /> The model weights and robot interface stay fixed.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button button-dark" href="#evaluation">Explore the findings <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><a class="button button-outline" href="#method">How it works <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="button button-text" href="#trajectories">Watch code in action <span aria-hidden="true">▷</span></a></div>
  </div>
  <figure class="hero-overview"><a href="/embodied-rsi/figures/embodied-rsi-overview.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the EmbodiedEvo overview at full resolution"><img src="/embodied-rsi/figures/embodied-rsi-overview.webp" alt="EmbodiedEvo overview: a coding agent develops Skills and Lessons through Playground feedback; a frozen harness is reused by fresh Test agents in RoboDojo" width="1668" height="744" fetchpriority="high" /></a><figcaption>Explore in Playground. Freeze the experience. Adapt local programs in new Test scenes.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="headline-metrics" aria-label="Key paper results">
    <a href="#evaluation"><span class="metric-kicker">PAIRED SUCCESS RATE</span><strong>8.50<span>→</span>30.17<small>%</small></strong><p>Astra/xhigh · task description</p></a>
    <a href="#transfer"><span class="metric-kicker">TRANSFER TO SOL / LUNA</span><strong>+8.86<span>/</span>+5.50<small>pp</small></strong><p>Astra experience vs. no experience</p></a>
    <a href="#cost"><span class="metric-kicker">TEST EXPENSE VS. RAW HISTORY</span><strong>53.0<small>% lower</small></strong><p>Skills & Lessons · token-based estimate</p></a>
  </div>
  <nav class="section-nav" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#method">Method</a><a href="#evaluation">Performance</a><a href="#capabilities">Capabilities</a><a href="#transfer">Transfer</a><a href="#experience">Experience</a><a href="#cost">Cost</a><a href="#trajectories">Demos</a></nav>
</section>
<section class="manifesto" aria-labelledby="idea-heading"><div class="container manifesto-inner"><p class="eyebrow">THE CENTRAL QUESTION</p><h2 id="idea-heading">How can physical interaction<br />improve <em>the next agent’s execution?</em></h2><p>A control program exposes targets, conditions, and recovery logic that an agent can inspect and revise. Physical feedback tests those choices. EmbodiedEvo retains the resulting procedures as <strong>Skills</strong> and their failure modes, constraints, and recovery guidance as <strong>Lessons</strong>, then evaluates their reuse on fresh scenes.</p><div class="manifesto-labels"><span>CODE AS THE CONTROL MEDIUM</span><span>AGENT-DIRECTED DEVELOPMENT</span><span>EXPERIENCE AS REUSABLE KNOWLEDGE</span></div></div></section>
<section id="method" class="section container" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
  <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow"><span>01 /</span> THE METHOD</p><h2 id="method-heading">Write. Execute. Learn.<br /><em>Then pass it on.</em></h2></div><p>Code makes control logic editable. The coding agent decides when to observe, execute, and recover. Skills and Lessons retain the resulting knowledge for independent agents on new scenes.</p></div>
  <div class="method-diagram">
    <div class="diagram-topline"><span class="eyebrow"><i class="status-dot"></i> PLAYGROUND</span><span class="diagram-note">EXPLORE · REVISE · RESET ALLOWED</span></div>
    <div class="inner-loop"><div class="diagram-node agent-node"><span class="node-icon">&lt;/&gt;</span><strong>Coding agent</strong><small>Observe & write Python</small></div><span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="diagram-node"><span class="node-icon">ƒ</span><strong>Executable program</strong><small>Loops, logic & control</small></div><span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="diagram-node"><span class="node-icon">⌘</span><strong>Robot environment</strong><small>Persistent simulation</small></div><span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="diagram-node feedback-node"><span class="node-icon">◉</span><strong>Execution feedback</strong><small>Images, output & success</small></div></div>
    <div class="inner-return"><span>↖</span><span>OBSERVE AGAIN · REVISE THE NEXT CODE SEGMENT</span><span>↵</span></div>
    <div class="outer-loop"><div><span class="eyebrow">THE EXPERIENCE LOOP</span><p>Reflect on interaction.<br /><strong>Update what the agent carries forward.</strong></p></div><span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="harness-box"><span>H<sub>k+1</sub></span><div><strong>Skills <code>procedures</code></strong><strong>Lessons <code>conditions</code></strong></div></div><span class="loop-back">↺ <span>REUSE IN THE<br />NEXT ATTEMPT</span></span></div>
    <div class="freeze-line"><span></span><b>↓ &nbsp; FREEZE SKILLS & LESSONS &nbsp; ↓</b><span></span></div>
    <div class="test-lane"><div><span class="eyebrow">TEST / TRANSFER</span><strong>Fresh scene. Fresh agent. Inherited experience.</strong></div><div class="test-lane-tags"><span>Read-only memory</span><span>Online feedback</span><span>No reset</span></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="method-interactive"><div class="method-steps" role="tablist" aria-label="Explore the improvement process">{stages.map((stage, i) => <button role="tab" id={`method-tab-${i}`} aria-controls="method-panel" aria-selected={i===0 ? 'true':'false'} tabindex={i===0 ? '0':'-1'} data-method-step={i}><span>{stage.number}</span>{stage.title}<span class="step-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></button>)}</div><div id="method-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="method-tab-0" tabindex="0"><span id="method-file" class="mono">{stages[0].file}</span><h3 id="method-title">{stages[0].word}</h3><p id="method-description">{stages[0].text}</p><div class="method-position" aria-hidden="true"><i class="active"></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div></div></div>
  <script id="method-data" type="application/json" set:html={JSON.stringify(stages)} />
  <div class="code-interface"><div><p class="eyebrow">WHY A CODING AGENT?</p><h3>Programs are<br /><em>the action interface.</em></h3><p>The agent emits executable Python segments. A segment can combine numerical reasoning, conditionals, loops, and reusable routines before calling the robot’s native controller.</p><p class="interface-emphasis">RoboDojo exposes RGB images and robot state. The native controller handles inverse kinematics and interpolation; object ground-truth poses, depth, and camera calibration are unavailable.</p></div><div class="interface-spec"><div class="spec-heading"><span>THE FIXED ENVIRONMENT INTERFACE</span><span>4 PRIMITIVES</span></div><div><code>get_instruction()</code><span>Read the task</span></div><div><code>get_observation()</code><span>Inspect the current state</span></div><div><code>step(action)</code><span>Execute native control</span></div><div><code>reset()</code><span class="playground-only">Playground only</span></div><p>Task instructions and primitives stay fixed. Skills and lessons are the evolving part of the harness.</p></div></div>
  <div class="protocol-grid">
    <article><span class="eyebrow">PLAYGROUND</span><h3>Experience can change.</h3><p>The simulator and Python state persist across submissions. The agent revises control code, Skills, and Lessons, and can reset the same scene to try again.</p></article>
    <article><span class="eyebrow">TEST</span><h3>Knowledge is frozen.</h3><p>Each scene gets a fresh agent, workspace, Python namespace, and simulator. Local programs still adapt through feedback. Inherited files are read-only, reset is disabled, and Test scenes share no new experience.</p></article>
    <article><span class="eyebrow">HELD FIXED</span><h3>The model and interface.</h3><p>Model weights, task goals, native primitives, and success conditions stay fixed. Each Playground chain and Test episode allows up to 100 code executions, subject to the task’s native-action allowance.</p></article>
  </div>
  <details class="paper-details"><summary>View the framework from the paper <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><figure class="paper-figure"><a href="/embodied-rsi/figures/embodied-rsi-method.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/embodied-rsi/figures/embodied-rsi-method.webp" alt="EmbodiedEvo framework: Playground interaction develops Skills and Lessons, which are frozen for independent interactive Test agents" width="1703" height="907" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>Playground develops reusable experience through physical feedback. Independent Test agents inherit the frozen harness and adapt their own local programs.</figcaption></figure></details>
</section>

<section id="evaluation" class="section evaluation-section" aria-labelledby="evaluation-heading"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow"><span>02 /</span> OVERALL PERFORMANCE</p><h2 id="evaluation-heading">The same agent.<br /><em>More useful experience.</em></h2></div><p>Paired tests hold the executor and Test scenes fixed. Inherited experience improves Astra’s task completion with both task descriptions alone and added wiki recipes.</p></div>
  <div class="results-board">
    <div class="results-title"><div><span class="badge badge-orange">PAIRED EVALUATION</span><h3>RoboDojo</h3><p>42 tasks · 5 scenes per task · 210 episodes per condition</p></div><span class="experiment-tag">GPT-6 ASTRA / XHIGH</span></div>
    <div class="input-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Task information in the paired evaluation">{results.inputs.map((input, i) => <button id={`input-tab-${input.id}`} role="tab" aria-controls={`input-panel-${input.id}`} aria-selected={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'} tabindex={i === 0 ? 0 : -1} data-input-tab={input.id}>{input.label}</button>)}</div>
    {results.inputs.map((input, i) => <div id={`input-panel-${input.id}`} class="input-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby={`input-tab-${input.id}`} tabindex="0" hidden={i !== 0}>
      <div class="paired-chart"><div class="chart-axis"><span>SUCCESS RATE (%)</span><span>0 → 100</span></div>
        {[{ name: 'No experience', value: input.baseline.success, kind: 'baseline' }, { name: 'Skills & Lessons', value: input.harness.success, kind: 'harness' }].map(row => <div class="paired-bar"><span>{row.name}</span><div class="bar-track"><i class={row.kind} style={`width:${row.value}%`}></i></div><strong>{fixed(row.value)}<small>%</small></strong></div>)}
        <p class="input-note">{input.note}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gain-callout"><span class="eyebrow">INHERITED EXPERIENCE</span><strong>+{fixed(input.gain)}<small>pp</small></strong><p>success rate</p>{input.interval ? <small>95% task-bootstrap interval<br />{fixed(input.interval[0])} to {fixed(input.interval[1])} pp</small> : <small>Experience adds a similar gain<br />with detailed task information.</small>}</div>
      <div class="results-table-wrap paired-table"><table><caption class="sr-only">Paired Astra/xhigh results with {input.label.toLowerCase()}</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Experience</th><th scope="col">Score ↑</th><th scope="col">Success rate ↑</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">No experience</th><td>{fixed(input.baseline.score)}</td><td>{fixed(input.baseline.success)}%</td></tr><tr class="ours-row"><th scope="row">Skills & Lessons</th><td>{fixed(input.harness.score)}</td><td>{fixed(input.harness.success)}%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    </div>)}
    <p class="results-caption">Score ranges from 0 to 100. Generalization variants, tasks within each category, and the five categories receive equal weight at their respective levels. The 95% interval uses 10,000 paired task-bootstrap draws and is conditional on the frozen harnesses.</p>
  </div>
  <noscript><p class="evidence-note">With task description + wiki, No Experience scores 20.67 / 13.36% success and Skills & Lessons score 42.42 / 35.08% success.</p></noscript>
  <div class="full-evaluation">
    <div class="full-summary"><span class="eyebrow">FULL EVALUATION / DESCRIPTION + WIKI</span><h3>2,100 episodes.<br /><em>Five kinds of challenge.</em></h3><div class="full-numbers"><div><strong>{fixed(results.fullEvaluation.score)}</strong><span>Average Score</span></div><div><strong>{fixed(results.fullEvaluation.success)}<small>%</small></strong><span>Success rate</span></div></div><p>Astra/xhigh inherits task-specific experience and runs on 50 scenes per task. Memory and Open have the highest success rates; Precision and Long-Horizon remain harder.</p></div>
    <div class="category-chart"><div class="chart-axis"><span>SUCCESS RATE BY CATEGORY</span><span>0 → 100%</span></div>{results.fullEvaluation.categories.map(category => <div class="category-row"><div><span>{category.name}</span><small>{category.tasks} tasks</small><strong>{fixed(category.success)}%</strong></div><div class="bar-track"><i style={`width:${category.success}%`}></i></div></div>)}</div>
  </div>
  <p class="evidence-note">The full evaluation uses one seed and target-task Playground exploration, including Open tasks. The wiki setting was selected after inspecting earlier outcomes, and its scene pool can overlap development scenes. The paper’s October 2, 2026 leaderboard snapshot retains different resource conditions across systems; the paired comparisons above isolate inherited experience.</p>
</div></section>

<section id="capabilities" class="section container" aria-labelledby="capability-heading">
  <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow"><span>03 /</span> CAPABILITY GAINS</p><h2 id="capability-heading">Better sequences.<br /><em>Contact still matters.</em></h2></div><p>Experience enables multi-stage and memory-dependent behavior. Gains coexist with regressions and tasks that remain unsolved under the evaluated budgets.</p></div>
  <div class="capability-cards"><article><span class="eyebrow">STACK BLOCKS</span><strong>0 → 100<small>%</small></strong><p>Paired success rate with Astra’s own experience.</p></article><article><span class="eyebrow">COVER BLOCKS / IMITATE SORTING</span><strong>0 → 80<small>%</small></strong><p>Each task improves with retained procedures and checks.</p></article><article class="boundary-card"><span class="eyebrow">REMAINING DIFFICULTIES</span><h3>Precision and contact.</h3><p>Tube insertion, key insertion, and table organization gain partial Score but have no successful scenes. Liquid pouring remains at zero Score.</p></article></div>
  <figure class="paper-figure qualitative-figure"><a href="/embodied-rsi/figures/experiment-qualitative.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the success, transfer, and regression figure at full resolution"><img src="/embodied-rsi/figures/experiment-qualitative.webp" alt="RoboDojo matched examples: Astra’s harness enables language stacking; Astra-produced experience helps Sol cover blocks; inherited experience regresses on elephant pickup" width="1192" height="988" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Success, transfer, and regression.</strong> Frames show independent runs under the labeled conditions. The stacking case completes a coordinated sequence; the pickup regression loses the object despite reaching the commanded poses. These selected cases illustrate behavior, not its prevalence.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="finding"><span class="eyebrow">WHAT CHANGES</span><p>Astra/xhigh has <strong>45 failure-to-success changes and 3 regressions</strong> across the paired episodes. A retained routine still needs observation checks and contact conditions that apply to the new scene.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="transfer" class="section transfer-section" aria-labelledby="transfer-heading"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow"><span>04 /</span> EXPERIENCE TRANSFER</p><h2 id="transfer-heading">One producer.<br /><em>Several receivers.</em></h2></div><p>Separate acquiring control knowledge from applying it. The same frozen Astra/xhigh harness can be reused by other executors on new scenes of the same task.</p></div>
  <div class="transfer-legend"><span><i class="legend-baseline"></i>No experience</span><span><i class="legend-self"></i>Own harness</span><span><i class="legend-transfer"></i>Astra/xhigh harness</span></div>
  <div class="transfer-grid">{results.executors.map(executor => <article class="transfer-card"><div class="transfer-card-heading"><h3>{executor.name}</h3><span>{executor.effort}</span></div><p>{executor.model}</p><div class="chart-axis"><span>SUCCESS RATE</span><span>0 → 40%</span></div>{[{ name: 'No experience', value: executor.baseline, kind: 'baseline' }, { name: 'Own harness', value: executor.self, kind: 'self' }, ...(executor.transferred === null ? [] : [{ name: 'Astra harness', value: executor.transferred, kind: 'transferred' }])].map(row => <div class="transfer-bar"><div><span>{row.name}</span><strong>{fixed(row.value)}%</strong></div><div class="bar-track"><i class={row.kind} style={`width:${row.value / 40 * 100}%`}></i></div></div>)}{executor.transferred === null && <p class="producer-label">SOURCE PRODUCER</p>}</article>)}</div>
  <div class="finding"><span class="eyebrow">PRODUCER QUALITY MATTERS</span><p>Sol and Luna gain no task-completion benefit from their own harnesses. Astra-produced experience raises their success rates by <strong>8.86 and 5.50 percentage points</strong> over no experience. Astra/medium also benefits from the higher-effort producer.</p></div>
  <div class="cross-task"><div><span class="eyebrow">ACROSS TASKS</span><h3>Useful knowledge can<br /><em>travel beyond a task label.</em></h3><p>On twelve fixed targets, both related and mismatched source tasks improve over no experience without new target-task exploration. The mismatched map outperforms the related map, motivating closer study of shared interface knowledge.</p></div><div class="results-table-wrap"><table class="compact-results"><caption>Cross-task study · Astra/xhigh · 12-target mean</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Experience source</th><th scope="col">Score</th><th scope="col">Success</th></tr></thead><tbody>{results.crossTask.rows.map(row => <tr><th scope="row">{row.name}</th><td>{fixed(row.score)}</td><td>{fixed(row.success)}%</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></div>
  <p class="evidence-note">Cross-task means weight the twelve targets equally and are separate from the 42-task benchmark average. These fixed source assignments do not estimate broad unseen-task generalization or isolate the contribution of interface knowledge.</p>
</div></section>

<section id="experience" class="section container" aria-labelledby="experience-heading">
  <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow"><span>05 /</span> EXPERIENCE REPRESENTATION</p><h2 id="experience-heading">Retain the correction.<br /><em>Retain when it works.</em></h2></div><p>Useful experience couples a procedure with its validity conditions. A failed attempt can teach a local rule; a successful routine can still fail under different contact geometry.</p></div>
  <figure class="paper-figure"><a href="/embodied-rsi/figures/experiment-experience-trace.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the recorded experience trace at full resolution"><img src="/embodied-rsi/figures/experiment-experience-trace.webp" alt="A dropped block in Playground leads to a retained bounded-carry rule; Test reuses the controller with an 8 mm translation bound and later completes the stack" width="1192" height="420" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>From a dropped load to a reused motion rule.</strong> Playground distinguishes a reached pose from a retained load. Test execution 9 uses the inherited controller with an 8 mm translation bound; the later terminal frame shows successful stacking at execution 15. The controller is abridged pseudocode; the lesson and call parameter come from actual artifacts.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="experience-cards"><article><span class="eyebrow">SKILLS</span><h3>How to act.</h3><p>Parameterized control procedures organize native actions around a subgoal. Bounded carries and measured-pose checks can be reused and composed in new local programs.</p></article><article><span class="eyebrow">LESSONS</span><h3>When to revise.</h3><p>Retained failures, contact conditions, and recovery guidance help the next agent decide whether a procedure applies. An accurate description of failure can still leave the next action unresolved.</p></article></div>
  <div class="finding"><span class="eyebrow">RAW HISTORY ALSO TRANSFERS</span><p>Exact Playground programs and public feedback reach <strong>29.78% success</strong>, compared with <strong>30.17%</strong> for Skills & Lessons. The 0.39-point difference has a 95% interval of −5.11 to 5.94 points. The structured representation’s measured advantage is lower Test-time model expense.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="cost" class="section cost-section" aria-labelledby="cost-heading"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow"><span>06 /</span> COST OF REUSE</p><h2 id="cost-heading">Similar success.<br /><em>Less history to consult.</em></h2></div><p>At a fixed Astra/xhigh executor and Playground source, Skills & Lessons reduce estimated Test expense by 53.0% relative to raw history. Acquiring and using experience still has a cost.</p></div>
  <div class="cost-cards">{results.representations.map(row => <article class:list={['cost-card', { 'cost-featured': row.id === 'structured' }]}><span class="eyebrow">{row.name}</span><strong>${row.expense.toFixed(3)}<small>/ episode</small></strong><div class="cost-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i style={`width:${row.expense / 12.203 * 100}%`}></i></div><p><b>{fixed(row.success)}%</b> success <span>·</span> {fixed(row.score)} Score</p></article>)}</div>
  <div class="acquisition"><div><span class="eyebrow">EXPERIENCE ACQUISITION / ASTRA XHIGH</span><h3>Reuse spreads the<br /><em>Playground cost.</em></h3><p>The source is acquired once and shared across its consumers. Astra’s self-harness costs more per Test episode than no experience ($5.732 vs. $4.058), so reuse alone does not create a cost crossover for this executor.</p></div><dl><div><dt>42 retained Playground runs</dt><dd>$1,213.84</dd></div><div><dt>Additional recorded archived attempts</dt><dd>$1,014.01</dd></div><div><dt>Known acquisition total</dt><dd>$2,227.85</dd></div></dl></div>
  <p class="evidence-note">Dollar amounts are estimates from recorded tokens and caching under a fixed rate schedule, not invoices. Test costs include failures. Missing historical usage makes known acquisition totals incomplete. Transferred Luna has lower per-episode expense ($0.188 vs. $0.239 without experience); total savings also depend on recovering the acquisition cost.</p>
</div></section>
<section id="trajectories" class="section container" aria-labelledby="trajectory-heading">
  <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow"><span>07 /</span> INTERACTIVE DEMONSTRATIONS</p><h2 id="trajectory-heading">Watch the code<br /><em>meet the world.</em></h2></div><p>Explore the coding interface through selected RoboTwin and RoboCasa Debug / Test recordings. The quantitative study above uses RoboDojo; these recordings illustrate online programming and recovery.</p></div>
  <div class="episode-picker" role="group" aria-label="Choose a benchmark trajectory">
    <button class="episode-button" data-episode="robodojo" aria-pressed="false"><span>RoboDojo</span><small>PRIMARY STUDY · TRACE BELOW</small></button>
    <button class="episode-button is-active" data-episode="robotwin" aria-pressed="true"><span>RoboTwin</span><small>STACK TWO BLOCKS <b>↗</b></small></button>
    <button class="episode-button" data-episode="robocasa" aria-pressed="false"><span>RoboCasa</span><small>OPEN A DRAWER <b>↗</b></small></button>
  </div>
  <div id="episode-loading" role="status" hidden>Loading the recorded trajectory…</div>
  <div id="episode-error" class="empty-state" role="alert" hidden><p>We couldn’t load this recording.</p><button class="button button-dark" id="retry-episode">Try again</button></div>
